---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const fixPathCode = String.raw`$userPath = [Environment]::GetEnvironmentVariable("Path","User")
$npmPath  = "$env:AppData\npm"
if ($userPath -notlike "*$npmPath*") {
    $newPath = if ([string]::IsNullOrEmpty($userPath)) { $npmPath } else { "$userPath;$npmPath" }
    [Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable("Path", $newPath, "User")
}`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Gemini CLI Getting Started & First MCP Integration — AI Agent Puzzle"
  description="From installing Gemini CLI to getting an API Key, locking the model, and connecting your first off-the-shelf MCP (using Notion as an example) — walk through the most common entry-level workflow step by step."
  lang="en"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container ai-agent-gemini-mcp">
      <a class="back-link" href="/">&larr; Back to Home</a>

      <header class="detail-header">
        <h1 class="detail-title">From Installing Gemini CLI to Connecting Your First Tool</h1>
        <p class="detail-subtitle">
          By now you already understand roughly how the LLM, Gemini CLI, MCP, and API divide their work. Next, we'll move forward one step: install Gemini CLI, open it, know where you're operating, then connect your first off-the-shelf MCP and confirm it works. As for OAuth, permissions, and deeper config-file edits — those can wait. Gemini CLI is Google's open-source AI agent designed to work with Gemini directly in the terminal, with built-in MCP extension support.
        </p>
        <p class="version-note">The steps and screens on this page were verified against Gemini CLI v0.55.1. npx always pulls the latest version, so the version number you see may be newer than the one here — small differences on screen are normal.</p>
      </header>

      <!-- Section: Prep -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">Just Two Things to Get Ready</h2>
      <div class="prep-grid">
        <div class="prep-card">
          <p class="prep-num">1</p>
          <h3 class="prep-title">A computer that can install and run Gemini CLI</h3>
          <p class="prep-desc">
            The official baseline requirements are: <strong>Node.js 20 or above</strong>, a usable shell (Bash, Zsh, or PowerShell), and an internet connection.
          </p>
          <p class="prep-desc">
            Recommended OS versions: macOS 15+, Windows 11 24H2+, Ubuntu 20.04+.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="prep-card">
          <p class="prep-num">2</p>
          <h3 class="prep-title">An account for the service you want to connect</h3>
          <p class="prep-desc">
            We'll use <strong>Notion</strong> as the first example here, because Notion has an official hosted MCP and explicitly positions it as a way for AI tools to securely access a Notion workspace.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Section: Check Node.js -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">First Check: Do You Have Node.js Installed?</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        If you plan to install Gemini CLI the most common way, the official docs require <strong>Node.js 20 or above</strong>. If you don't know whether you have it, type this in your terminal first:
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block"><code>node -v</code></pre>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        If a version number appears and it's 20.x or higher, this step is basically fine. If there's no version number, or the version is too old, install or update Node.js before moving on.
      </p>

      <div class="faq-list">
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary class="faq-summary">Supplement: Node.js installation steps (Windows example)</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <ol class="step-list">
              <li class="step-item">
                <span class="step-num">1</span>
                <div class="step-body">
                  <h4 class="step-title">Confirm your computer's architecture first</h4>
                  <p class="step-desc">
                    Check via "Settings → System → About → System type". If you see a typical Intel or AMD CPU, almost always pick <strong>x64</strong>.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </li>
              <li class="step-item">
                <span class="step-num">2</span>
                <div class="step-body">
                  <h4 class="step-title">Open the official download page and grab the matching Windows installer</h4>
                  <p class="step-desc">
                    Go to the Node.js official download page, find the LTS version (currently 24.15.0, the version updates over time), and download based on your architecture:
                  </p>
                  <ul class="check-list check-list--compact">
                    <li>Most people: <code>node-v24.15.0-x64.msi</code></li>
                    <li>ARM devices: <code>node-v24.15.0-arm64.msi</code></li>
                  </ul>
                </div>
              </li>
              <li class="step-item">
                <span class="step-num">3</span>
                <div class="step-body">
                  <h4 class="step-title">Run the .msi to install</h4>
                  <p class="step-desc">
                    After downloading, double-click the .msi file and follow the install wizard. In general, accept all defaults unless you have a special need — that's the least error-prone path.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </li>
              <li class="step-item">
                <span class="step-num">4</span>
                <div class="step-body">
                  <h4 class="step-title">How to confirm the install succeeded</h4>
                  <p class="step-desc">
                    After installing, close the existing PowerShell / CMD, open a fresh terminal window, then type:
                  </p>
                  <pre class="code-block"><code>node -v
npm -v</code></pre>
                  <p class="step-desc">
                    If you installed the latest LTS, <code>node -v</code> should show something like <code>v24.15.0</code>, and the npm version that ships with it is <code>11.12.1</code>.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </li>
              <li class="step-item">
                <span class="step-num">5</span>
                <div class="step-body">
                  <h4 class="step-title">One more simple test</h4>
                  <p class="step-desc">In PowerShell, type:</p>
                  <pre class="code-block"><code>node</code></pre>
                  <p class="step-desc">If it works, you'll enter Node.js's interactive mode. Then type:</p>
                  <pre class="code-block"><code>console.log("Hello Node.js")</code></pre>
                  <p class="step-desc">After seeing the output, type:</p>
                  <pre class="code-block"><code>.exit</code></pre>
                  <p class="step-desc">This means Node.js itself is running properly.</p>
                </div>
              </li>
            </ol>

            <div class="sub-card">
              <p class="sub-card__label">◇ What is npm, and why does it install alongside?</p>
              <p class="sub-card__text">
                npm is the standard package manager in the Node.js ecosystem, used to install libraries and tools. The Node.js official download page notes that the installer ships with npm, so you usually don't need to install it separately. For example, you'll often use:
              </p>
              <pre class="code-block"><code>npm init -y
npm install express</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="sub-card">
              <p class="sub-card__label">◇ Common issues and how to fix them</p>
              <ol class="trouble-list">
                <li>
                  <strong>"'node' is not recognized as an internal or external command"</strong>
                  <p>This usually means the new environment variables haven't been picked up by the current terminal. Try these in order:</p>
                  <ol class="trouble-sub" type="a">
                    <li>Close PowerShell / CMD and reopen</li>
                    <li>If that still fails, log out or restart</li>
                    <li>If still no luck, verify Node.js was actually fully installed</li>
                  </ol>
                  <p>You can also check whether this folder exists: <code>C:\Program Files\nodejs\</code>. If <code>node.exe</code> is there but the command still doesn't work, the PATH usually didn't take effect properly.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <strong>Is my computer ARM or x64?</strong>
                  <p>Most Windows laptops and desktops are x64. Only pick ARM64 if you specifically know you have a Windows on ARM device. Node.js officially provides both Windows installers.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <strong>Do I need to verify the download wasn't tampered with?</strong>
                  <p>If you care about security, the Node.js official download page provides SHASUMS verification info and instructions; v24.15.0 has a corresponding SHASUMS256.txt to compare against. In PowerShell you can use:</p>
                  <pre class="code-block"><code>Get-FileHash .\node-v24.15.0-x64.msi -Algorithm SHA256</code></pre>
                  <p>Then compare the result against the value in the official SHASUMS256.txt.</p>
                </li>
              </ol>
            </div>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>

      <!-- Section: PowerShell execution policy -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">Windows Users: Open Up the PowerShell Execution Policy First</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        On a fresh Windows account, the PowerShell execution policy defaults to <code>Restricted</code>, which blocks <code>npm.ps1</code> and <code>gemini.ps1</code>. Skip this step and the next one — installing — will get stuck.
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        The error message looks like this:
      </p>

      <figure class="figure">
        <img
          class="figure__img"
          src="/learning/agent/PowerShell執行原則錯誤.png"
          alt="PowerShell error showing npm.ps1 cannot be loaded because running scripts is disabled on this system"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="figure__caption">The error npm returns when script execution is disabled</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        The fix is one line in PowerShell — no administrator rights needed:
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block"><code>Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope CurrentUser -ExecutionPolicy RemoteSigned</code></pre>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Type <code>Y</code> at the confirmation prompt. Then you can verify:
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block"><code>Get-ExecutionPolicy -Scope CurrentUser   # should show RemoteSigned
npm -v</code></pre>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          <strong>What that line actually does:</strong> <code>RemoteSigned</code> means scripts you wrote locally can run, while scripts downloaded from the internet need a digital signature. This is Microsoft's recommended setting for ordinary use — it isn't turning security off. And <code>-Scope CurrentUser</code> only affects the current account, not the whole machine.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Section: Install Gemini CLI -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">Installing Gemini CLI: It Depends Which Computer You're On</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Pick one of the two routes below based on the machine in front of you. The difference isn't difficulty — it's whether you have permission to install things on that computer.
      </p>

      <div class="install-grid">
        <article class="install-card">
          <p class="install-tag">Route A</p>
          <h3 class="install-title">Your own laptop</h3>
          <p class="install-desc">
            If the computer is yours, installing it properly is the least hassle:
          </p>
          <pre class="code-block"><code>Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope CurrentUser -ExecutionPolicy RemoteSigned
npm install -g @google/gemini-cli
gemini --version</code></pre>
          <p class="install-desc">
            After that, typing <code>gemini</code> is all it takes to launch.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="install-card">
          <p class="install-tag">Route B</p>
          <h3 class="install-title">A computer lab, or a machine with a restore card</h3>
          <p class="install-desc">
            No administrator rights required. This route needs no install, no PATH fixing, and leaves no trace after a reboot:
          </p>
          <pre class="code-block"><code>Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope CurrentUser -ExecutionPolicy RemoteSigned
npx @google/gemini-cli</code></pre>
          <p class="install-desc">
            The first npx run asks whether you want to download the package:
          </p>

          <figure class="figure">
            <img
              class="figure__img"
              src="/learning/agent/npx首次執行確認.png"
              alt="npx asking whether to download the package on its first run"
              loading="lazy"
            />
            <figcaption class="figure__caption">The download confirmation on the first npx run</figcaption>
          </figure>

          <p class="install-desc">
            Type <code>y</code> and press Enter to start the download. This only appears the first time — after that the cache means it launches straight away.
          </p>
          <p class="install-desc">
            Wherever the rest of this material shows a <code>gemini</code> command, substitute <code>npx @google/gemini-cli</code> — the subcommand syntax is identical.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-list">
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary class="faq-summary">Route A note: if you get "'gemini' is not recognized"</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>This is common on a new account. The package really did install, into <code>%AppData%\npm</code> — that path just isn't in your user PATH.</p>
            <p>First confirm the package is actually there:</p>
            <pre class="code-block"><code>npm config get prefix
Test-Path "$env:AppData\npm\gemini.ps1"</code></pre>
            <p>Then add the path — again, no administrator rights needed:</p>
            <pre class="code-block"><code>{fixPathCode}</code></pre>
            <p>After changing it, close PowerShell and open a fresh window for it to take effect.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary class="faq-summary">Route B note: the first npx run is slower</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>npx downloads the package on first run and caches it in <code>%LocalAppData%\npm-cache\_npx</code>, and in a lab with restore cards every reboot means downloading it again.</p>
            <p>This route can also lock the model straight from a flag, so you don't need a <code>.env</code> at all:</p>
            <pre class="code-block"><code>npx @google/gemini-cli --model gemini-3.5-flash-lite</code></pre>
            <p>Subcommands work the same way, for example:</p>
            <pre class="code-block"><code>npx @google/gemini-cli mcp add --transport http notion https://mcp.notion.com/mcp</code></pre>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary class="faq-summary">Installation methods for other systems</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>If you're on macOS or Linux and already use Homebrew, you can use:</p>
            <pre class="code-block"><code>brew install gemini-cli</code></pre>
            <p>If you're on macOS and prefer MacPorts, you can use:</p>
            <pre class="code-block"><code>sudo port install gemini-cli</code></pre>
            <p>If your environment is more constrained, the official docs also offer an Anaconda route: first create a conda environment that includes nodejs, then use npm inside that environment to install Gemini CLI.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>

      <!-- Section: Get an API Key -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">Before the First Launch, Go Get an API Key</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        As of June 18, 2026, Gemini CLI no longer offers Google sign-in for free personal accounts. The old "open a browser and log in with your Google account" path is fully gone; you now use a Gemini API Key instead.
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        The steps:
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block"><code>1. Sign in to Google AI Studio with your Google account
2. Click Get API Key at the bottom left (the key icon)
3. Click Create API Key at the top right
4. Once it's created, copy the API Key</code></pre>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          <strong>You must use a personal Gmail account, not a school one.</strong> School Google Workspace accounts usually have "create a Google Cloud project" disabled, which surfaces as <code>Project creation is disabled</code>, and there's no way around it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">While you're there, check your quota</h3>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Once you have the key, it's worth a quick look at the rate-limit page to confirm things will actually run:
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block"><code>1. Click Rate Limit on the left to see your current account's quota
2. Click "See more" to expand the full list
3. Confirm the Gemini 3.5 Flash Lite row reads 15 RPM / 250K TPM / 500 RPD</code></pre>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          <strong>RPM (Requests Per Minute):</strong> how many requests you can send per minute — a quota of 15. It doesn't zero out on the minute mark; it continuously counts what you've used over the past 60 seconds, so if you run out at 14:30:10, you're clear again at 14:31:10. In practice one rule is enough: if you hit it, wait a minute.
        </p>
        <p class="callout-text">
          <strong>TPM (Tokens Per Minute):</strong> how many input tokens you can send per minute — a quota of 250,000. It resets the same rolling-60-second way RPM does.
        </p>
        <p class="callout-text">
          <strong>RPD (Requests Per Day):</strong> how many requests you can send per day in total — a quota of 500. It resets at midnight Pacific time, which works out to 15:00 Taiwan time (16:00 from November through March, because of the US time change). Hitting RPD means waiting until the next day — unlike RPM, where a minute is enough.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          <strong>One instruction ≠ one request.</strong> Say you type "summarize this Notion note for me" — behind the scenes, the agent runs:
        </p>
        <ol class="callout-list">
          <li>Sends the instruction → request 1</li>
          <li>Model decides to call notion-search → request 2 (search results go back to the model)</li>
          <li>Model decides to call notion-fetch → request 3</li>
          <li>Model reads the content and produces a summary → request 4</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="callout-text">
          So a single sentence can easily be 4–10 requests.
        </p>
        <p class="callout-text">
          <strong>TPM can run out before RPM does.</strong> Every request resends the entire conversation history as input, so the longer the conversation, the more tokens each single request eats.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">Create a test project and the config file</h3>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Start with a clean test folder, then write the config file into it:
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block"><code>mkdir C:\gemini-class-test
cd C:\gemini-class-test
mkdir .gemini
Set-Content -Path ".gemini\.env" -Value "GEMINI_API_KEY=your-key`nGEMINI_MODEL=gemini-3.5-flash-lite"</code></pre>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">Verify the file was actually created correctly</h3>
      <pre class="code-block"><code>Get-ChildItem .gemini -Force
Get-Content .gemini\.env</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        The fallback model is <code>gemini-3.1-flash-lite-preview</code>, with the same quota. Note that 3.1 needs the <code>-preview</code> suffix and 3.5 doesn't — they're different models, and getting it wrong won't quietly fall back. If you're on Route B, you can skip <code>.env</code> entirely and pass <code>--model</code> at launch instead.
      </p>

      <!-- Section: After install -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">After Installing, Confirm It Actually Opens</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Once that's ready, you can simply type:
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block"><code>gemini                      # Route A
npx @google/gemini-cli      # Route B</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        The first time you launch inside this folder, a confirmation box appears: <strong>Do you trust the files in this folder?</strong>
      </p>

      <figure class="figure">
        <img
          class="figure__img"
          src="/learning/agent/資料夾信任提示.png"
          alt="Gemini CLI asking whether you trust the files in this folder on first launch"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="figure__caption">The folder-trust prompt on first launch inside a folder</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        The box lists which settings from this folder will be loaded. <strong>Right now it's empty</strong>, because we haven't added any MCP yet. Once you've finished "Add Your First Off-the-Shelf MCP" below and come back to this screen, there'll be an extra line — <code>MCP Servers (1): notion</code> — and that's when you'll see concretely what "trusting this folder" actually means.
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Of the three options, pick <strong>1. Trust folder (gemini-class-test)</strong>.
      </p>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          If you pick 3 (Don't trust), the CLI won't read the <code>.env</code> or the MCP settings in this folder, and every step after this will fail.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Next, you'll see three authentication options:
      </p>
      <ol class="login-list">
        <li><span class="login-letter">1</span><span class="login-text">Sign in with Google</span></li>
        <li><span class="login-letter">2</span><span class="login-text">Use Gemini API Key</span></li>
        <li><span class="login-letter">3</span><span class="login-text">Vertex AI</span></li>
      </ol>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Pick <strong>2. Use Gemini API Key</strong>. The first option is still on screen, but it no longer serves free personal accounts — choosing it just leads down a path that doesn't go anywhere. The <code>.env</code> you set up earlier exists precisely for this step.
      </p>

      <figure class="figure">
        <img
          class="figure__img"
          src="/learning/agent/驗證方式選單.png"
          alt="Gemini CLI's authentication method menu"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="figure__caption">
          The authentication menu. The line below it, <code>Existing API key detected (GEMINI_API_KEY)</code>, means the CLI has already read the <code>.env</code> you just created
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        After you choose, one more confirmation screen appears — and it <strong>prints your API Key straight onto the screen</strong>. Press Enter to finish.
      </p>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          One reminder while we're here: the key is shown in plain text. If you're projecting, recording, or sharing your screen with someone, switch away for this step. A leaked key means someone else can spend your quota — and you won't notice right away.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          So if you see the menu after first launch, don't panic. <strong>This isn't a stuck state — it's part of the normal flow.</strong> If you land on the screen below after choosing, everything up to here went right.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="figure">
        <img
          class="figure__img"
          src="/learning/agent/成功進入GeminiCLI.png"
          alt="Terminal screen after successfully entering Gemini CLI, with the workspace, sandbox and model status bar along the bottom"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="figure__caption">Successfully entering Gemini CLI</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Check the model in the bottom-right corner. If it reads <code>gemini-3.5-flash-lite</code> — the one you set earlier — your <code>.env</code> has taken effect.
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        That bottom row carries two more pieces of information: on the left is your current working folder, in the middle is the sandbox status. <strong>Check that the path on the left is <code>C:\gemini-class-test</code></strong> — if it isn't, you launched from a different folder, and neither the <code>.env</code> nor the MCP settings you add later will be read.
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        The <code>1 MCP server</code> in the top right of the screenshot is there because this image was captured after the MCP steps further down were already done. Yours should still be empty at this point; it'll show up once you finish the next section.
      </p>

      <div class="faq-list">
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary class="faq-summary">If you can't open Gemini CLI, check these first</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <ol class="trouble-list">
              <li>
                <p>First check whether your <strong>Node.js version</strong> is too old. The official docs explicitly require Node.js 20.0.0 or above.</p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>Confirm you haven't confused <strong>install</strong> with <strong>run</strong>.</p>
                <p><code>npm install -g @google/gemini-cli</code> installs it; <code>gemini</code> launches it.</p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>If you're using npx, make sure your terminal can reach the internet, since it needs to fetch the package.</p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>If you've already opened it but don't know which option to take, just remember: pick <strong>Use Gemini API Key</strong>, not Sign in with Google.</p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>

      <!-- Section: Where am I typing -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">First, Work Out Where You're Typing</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Two kinds of commands are coming up. They look alike, but they belong in different places. Learning to tell where you are right now saves a lot of wasted effort.
      </p>

      <div class="info-table-wrapper">
        <table class="info-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>What you see</th><th>Where you are</th><th>What you can type</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>White-on-black <code>PS C:\...&gt;</code>, no logo</td>
              <td>PowerShell</td>
              <td><code>gemini</code>, <code>gemini mcp add</code>, <code>gemini --resume</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>A Gemini logo, with a status bar and input box at the bottom</td>
              <td>Inside Gemini CLI</td>
              <td><code>/mcp list</code>, <code>/mcp auth</code>, <code>/stats</code>, <code>/quit</code></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        To leave Gemini CLI and get back to PowerShell, type <code>/quit</code>, or press <code>Ctrl+C</code> twice.
      </p>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          Typing in the wrong place doesn't raise an error. If you type <code>gemini mcp add</code> inside Gemini CLI, it just treats the line as conversation and answers you politely — it looks perfectly normal, but the command never ran.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Section: Warm-up before MCP -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">Warm-up Before Using MCP</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Once you've finished these few things:
      </p>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>Gemini CLI is installed</li>
        <li>Gemini CLI opens</li>
        <li>You've authenticated with an API Key, and the model is locked to Flash Lite</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        That's the right moment to start connecting your first off-the-shelf MCP. Because by this point, you already know you're operating inside the Gemini CLI workspace — instead of jumping straight into a pile of external configuration from the start.
      </p>

      <div class="highlight-card">
        <p class="highlight-text">
          This order matters. Because what you're about to do isn't "turn Gemini CLI into Notion" — it's <strong>add a tool entry point from Gemini CLI to Notion</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        The official Gemini CLI docs describe an MCP server as a bridge to external systems, APIs, or data sources; Notion's official docs provide a hosted MCP endpoint, letting MCP-aware AI tools connect into Notion.
      </p>

      <!-- Section: Where MCP settings live -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">Where MCP Settings Get Written</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        When you use <code>gemini mcp add</code> to add a new MCP server, Gemini CLI writes the configuration into <code>settings.json</code>. There are two common locations:
      </p>

      <div class="scope-grid">
        <article class="scope-card">
          <p class="scope-card__label">User level</p>
          <p class="scope-card__path"><code>~/.gemini/settings.json</code></p>
          <p class="scope-card__desc">
            More like "the whole computer remembers it." Only consider this level if you want to use the same MCP across many places in the future.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="scope-card">
          <p class="scope-card__label">Project level</p>
          <p class="scope-card__path"><code>.gemini/settings.json</code></p>
          <p class="scope-card__desc">
            More like "only this folder's project remembers it." If you just want to try connecting an MCP within a single project, this level is safer.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          When you're starting out, <strong>using project level to understand this concept is usually steadier than going straight to a global setup</strong>.
        </p>
        <p class="callout-text">
          The good news: <code>gemini mcp add</code> writes to the project-level <code>.gemini/settings.json</code> by default, so following the steps below won't accidentally change settings for your whole computer.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Section: Add first MCP -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">Add Your First Off-the-Shelf MCP</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Once you're ready, you can add your first MCP in the terminal. The official Gemini CLI syntax for adding an HTTP-type MCP is:
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block"><code>gemini mcp add --transport http &lt;name&gt; &lt;url&gt;</code></pre>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">Why HTTP here?</h3>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        MCP servers come in different connection types — common ones include stdio, HTTP, and SSE. We start with HTTP here because Notion provides a remote endpoint, and connecting it directly via HTTP is the most intuitive. As a beginner, knowing "different tools use different connection types" is enough:
      </p>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li><strong>stdio</strong>: common for small tools launched locally</li>
        <li><strong>HTTP</strong>: common for remote services</li>
        <li><strong>SSE</strong>: also a remote connection type — for now, just knowing the name is enough</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        So using Notion as our first example, and given that Notion's official MCP endpoint is <code>https://mcp.notion.com/mcp</code>, you can do this:
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block"><code>gemini mcp add --transport http notion https://mcp.notion.com/mcp                      # Route A
npx @google/gemini-cli mcp add --transport http notion https://mcp.notion.com/mcp      # Route B</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        This line goes in <strong>PowerShell</strong>, not inside Gemini CLI. If you're not sure where you are, look back at the "where you're typing right now" table above.
      </p>

      <ol class="step-list">
        <li class="step-item">
          <span class="step-num">1</span>
          <div class="step-body">
            <h3 class="step-title">Finish adding</h3>
            <p class="step-desc">
              On success you'll see <code>MCP server "notion" added to project settings. (http)</code>. You can then read the config file yourself to confirm what was written:
            </p>
            <pre class="code-block"><code>Get-Content .gemini\settings.json</code></pre>
            <figure class="figure">
              <img
                class="figure__img"
                src="/learning/agent/新增MCP成功_npx.png"
                alt="The success message from mcp add, and the settings.json contents read back with Get-Content"
                loading="lazy"
              />
              <figcaption class="figure__caption">The config really did land in the project-level <code>.gemini\settings.json</code></figcaption>
            </figure>
            <p class="step-desc">
              Note the <code>"type": "http"</code> — the CLI generates that for you, you don't write it by hand. Some older tutorials online write <code>"httpUrl"</code> instead; that's the old syntax and it won't connect any more.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="step-item">
          <span class="step-num">2</span>
          <div class="step-body">
            <h3 class="step-title">Restart Gemini CLI and check the current MCP status first</h3>
            <p class="step-desc">After restarting Gemini CLI, type:</p>
            <pre class="code-block"><code>/mcp list</code></pre>
            <p class="step-desc">You should see notion listed as not connected.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="step-item">
          <span class="step-num">3</span>
          <div class="step-body">
            <h3 class="step-title">Complete OAuth, then check the MCP status again</h3>
            <pre class="code-block"><code>/mcp auth notion</code></pre>
            <p class="step-desc">This line goes inside Gemini CLI. Running it opens your browser automatically:</p>
            <figure class="figure">
              <img
                class="figure__img"
                src="/learning/agent/Notion授權畫面.png"
                alt="The Notion MCP browser authorization screen"
                loading="lazy"
              />
              <figcaption class="figure__caption">The Notion MCP authorization screen</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <p class="step-desc">Three things on this screen deserve attention:</p>
            <ul class="check-list check-list--compact">
              <li><strong>Pick the workspace at the top first.</strong> If your Notion account has more than one workspace, picking the wrong one means you'll find nothing later.</li>
              <li><strong>The four lines in the middle are the permissions you're granting</strong> — worth pausing on. It isn't just "read your pages": it also covers acting on your behalf, and viewing workspace members and their email addresses. This is what we'll come back to when we talk about least privilege.</li>
              <li><strong>The <code>I recognize and trust this URL</code> checkbox in the yellow box must be ticked.</strong> Without it the Continue button below stays grey and can't be clicked. The <code>localhost:52850</code> number in that box differs per person and per run — it doesn't need to match the screenshot.</li>
            </ul>

            <p class="step-desc">Once authorization is done, check again:</p>
            <pre class="code-block"><code>/mcp list</code></pre>
            <p class="step-desc">The status goes from Disconnected to Ready, followed by the full list of tools this MCP provides.</p>
            <figure class="figure">
              <img
                class="figure__img"
                src="/learning/agent/MCP工具清單.png"
                alt="/mcp list showing notion as ready, with 28 tools and 2 resources listed"
                loading="lazy"
              />
              <figcaption class="figure__caption"><code>/mcp list</code> after authorization</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <p class="step-desc">Two things here are worth a look.</p>
            <p class="step-desc">
              First, tool names in the list take the form <code>mcp_notion_notion-search</code>. The first two layers are prefixes Gemini CLI adds itself (<code>mcp_</code> means it came from an MCP, <code>notion_</code> means which server it came from). The tool's own name is the last part — <code>notion-search</code>. Later material refers to tools simply as <code>notion-search</code> or <code>notion-fetch</code>; they're the same things you're seeing here.
            </p>
            <p class="step-desc">
              Second, what you just connected in one go is <strong>28 tools</strong>. That's far more than this course will use, and the description of every one of them is sent to the model alongside each question you ask. In "Least Privilege &amp; Security Boundaries" we'll look at how to narrow this down to just the few you actually need.
            </p>
            <p class="step-desc step-desc--alert">
              One last reminder: this connection isn't permanent. For safety, it expires after a period of disuse. If notion shows as Disconnected next time you open up, you haven't done anything wrong — just run <code>/mcp auth notion</code> again.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>

    </div>
  </section>
